course: biology
lesson: 183
title: Mountains of the world: snow leopards, high-flying geese, condors and Everest spiders
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The higher you climb a mountain, the colder it gets and the thinner the air becomes, so each breath brings less oxygen; climbers on Everest often carry bottles of oxygen.
explain: Mountain animals have bodies made for this: thick fur and long tails against the cold, big lungs and special blood that grabs oxygen from thin air.
explain: Picture a goat standing on a cliff so steep you could not climb it with your hands: its hooves have soft pads that grip like the soles of trainers and hard edges that dig in.
explain: Even near the top of Mount Everest, where nothing grows, a tiny jumping spider lives by eating insects that the wind blows up from below.
fact: Snow leopards live high in the mountains of Asia; their long thick tails help them balance and wrap round their faces to keep them warm when they sleep.
fact: Snow leopards cannot roar like lions and tigers; they hiss, growl and make a puffing sound instead.
fact: Yaks have two horns, thick long hair and big lungs for the thin, cold air of the Himalayas, and people use them to carry loads to very high places.
fact: Bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas, the highest mountains on Earth; their blood carries oxygen very well, and they often fly at night or early morning, when the air is cooler and denser.
fact: The Andean condor is one of the heaviest flying birds; it glides on rising warm air, and one was recorded flying over a hundred and seventy kilometres without flapping.
fact: The Himalayan jumping spider lives over six thousand metres above sea level, higher than almost any other animal, eating insects blown up by the wind.
fact: Mountain goats and ibexes climb steep cliffs; their hooves have soft pads and hard edges, and Alpine ibexes even climb dam walls to lick salt from the stones.
fact: Giant pandas live in cool mountain forests in China and eat mostly bamboo, which has little energy, so they eat for many hours a day; a special wrist bone works like a thumb to grip it.
fact: Llamas and alpacas live in the Andes; their blood carries oxygen well, and they spit mostly at each other to settle arguments.
fact: The red panda lives in mountain forests and eats bamboo too, wrapping its long fluffy tail around itself for warmth, but it is not a close relative of the giant panda.
fact: Mountain flowers grow low and are often hairy to keep warm against strong sun, cold winds and short summers, and some grow only a few millimetres a year.
fact: Marmots whistle loudly when they see an eagle or a fox, warning the others to run to their burrows, and they sleep through winter for up to eight months.
exercise: Why does a snow leopard have a long thick tail? | For balance and to wrap round its face for warmth | To catch fish in the icy mountain streams with one swipe | To store fat for winter | To swat flies
exercise: Which sound can a snow leopard NOT make? | A roar | A loud hiss | A growl | A soft puff
exercise: What helps yaks live high in the Himalayas? | Thick long hair and big lungs | Thin skin and short legs for running | Wings for flying over the snow and ice | A hump full of water, like a camel
exercise: What do people use yaks for in the mountains? | Carrying loads to very high places | Racing on ice | Guarding sheep from wolves by roaring | Pulling boats on rivers
exercise: How can bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas? | Their blood carries oxygen very well | They carry oxygen in their feathers | They fly only in hot midday air | They hold their breath the whole way
exercise: When do bar-headed geese often fly over the mountains? | At night or early morning, when the air is cooler and denser | Only at midday in summer, when the sun is hottest and the air is thin | Only during snowstorms | Only when the moon is full
exercise: How does an Andean condor stay up for hours? | It glides on rising warm air | It flaps its wings very fast | It rests on clouds | It is lighter than air
exercise: What does the spider near the top of Everest eat? | Insects blown up the mountain by the wind | Snow and ice | Moss and lichens that grow between the rocks up there | Eagle eggs
exercise: How high does the Himalayan jumping spider live? | Over six thousand metres above sea level | Only at the foot of the mountain, in the warm forests | Just above a beach | Under the snow at sea level
exercise: What helps mountain goats grip steep cliffs? | Hooves with soft pads and hard edges | Sticky toes like a gecko's that cling to rock | Long claws like a cat | Hooks on their knees
exercise: Why do Alpine ibexes climb dam walls? | To lick salt from the stones | To look for water hidden in cracks | To escape from people down below | To find a warm place to sleep
exercise: Why do giant pandas eat for many hours a day? | Bamboo has little energy | They are always cold in the mountains | They also eat at night to grow horns | They store food in their cheeks
exercise: What helps a giant panda grip bamboo? | A special wrist bone that works like a thumb | A long sticky tongue that it wraps round the stems | Hooks on its tail | Its teeth alone
exercise: Why do llamas spit? | Mostly to settle arguments with other llamas | To cool down in the sun on very hot days in summer | To clean their faces | To water plants
exercise: Which is true about the red panda? | It eats bamboo but is not a close relative of the giant panda | It is a baby giant panda | It is a kind of fox that eats fish | It lives in the desert
exercise: Why do many mountain flowers grow low and hairy? | To keep warm against cold winds | To hide from the hot sun at noon in summer | To catch birds that land on them | To grow faster than the trees around them
exercise: Why does a marmot whistle loudly? | To warn others that an eagle or fox is near | To call its babies out of the burrow for lunch | To scare away snow | To sing in spring
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Tai Lung the snow leopard roars like a lion. What is wrong with that? | Real snow leopards cannot roar; they hiss, growl and puff | Snow leopards only meow like house cats | Snow leopards have no voice at all | Nothing; snow leopards roar louder than lions
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Master Shifu is a red panda and Po is a giant panda. «so shifu is po's little cousin innit» What is right? | No, red pandas and giant pandas are not close relatives, though both eat bamboo | Yes, a red panda is a young giant panda | Yes, they are the same animal in two colours | No, red pandas eat only meat
test: In The Emperor's New Groove the emperor is turned into a llama. If a real llama spat, who would it most likely spit at? | Another llama, to settle an argument | Any person at all who comes close to it | Only at the moon, when it is full and bright | At its own food, to make it soft to chew
test: «4 yaks pulling a load, how many horns and tails altogether» | 12 | 8 | 4 | 16
test: «2 snow leopards and 3 marmots, count the tails» | 5 | 2 | 3 | 10
test: What would happen to a lowland goose that tried to fly as high as a bar-headed goose? | It would struggle to get enough oxygen from the thin air | It would fly even faster | It would be warmer up there | It would turn into a bar-headed goose, which can fly very high
test: «why's it so hard to breathe up here, the air's still there innit» What is right? | The air is thinner high up, so each breath holds less oxygen | There is no air at all on mountains | The air is too hot to breathe | Mountains have more oxygen than the beach
test: A hiker hears a sharp whistle and sees small furry animals rush into holes. What happened? | A marmot spotted an eagle or fox and warned the others | Someone was calling a dog | The marmots were playing tag | It was time for their lunch, and the oldest marmot was calling them in
test: «a panda eats bamboo all day, what a lazy fatso» What is right? | Bamboo has so little energy that pandas must eat for many hours to get enough | Pandas eat all day because bamboo is too tasty | Pandas eat little and sleep the rest | Pandas also hunt yaks
test: Why might a condor choose to fly in the warm middle of the day? | Rising warm air lets it glide without flapping | It cannot see anything in the early morning | Insects are easier to catch then | The wind stops at midday
test: Why can a jumping spider live where no plants grow, near the top of Everest? | The wind carries insects up the mountain for it to eat | It eats rocks | It does not need any food at all for months | It flies down every day to hunt in the valleys far below
exam: Identify the principal difficulty presented to animals by the atmosphere at high altitude. | Each breath contains less oxygen owing to the thinness of the air | The air contains an excess of oxygen | The air is warmer than at sea level, and warm air is harder to breathe | The air is devoid of all gases
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the snow leopard? | It inhabits the high mountains of Asia and is incapable of roaring | It inhabits the African savanna and roars loudly | It is a close relative of the giant panda | It hibernates for eight months annually
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bar-headed goose. | Its blood transports oxygen with great efficiency, permitting flight over the Himalayas | It is incapable of flight at any altitude | It traverses mountains exclusively at midday | It carries reserves of oxygen within its feathers
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the giant panda devotes many hours each day to feeding. | Bamboo yields comparatively little energy | It is compelled to store food for hibernation | It consumes chiefly meat, which is scarce | Its teeth are unable to chew rapidly
